WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.642
Mary knew that Dickon had meant the picture of the nesting bird to be a message.

2
00:00:19.842 --> 00:00:21.943
It was a promise that he would keep her secret.

3
00:00:23.324 --> 00:00:25.865
She was the bird, and the garden was her nest.

4
00:00:27.005 --> 00:00:29.526
Mary hoped that Dickon would come back the next day.

5
00:00:31.107 --> 00:00:33.808
She fell asleep that night looking forward to the morning.

6
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.022
But she was awakened in the night by the sound of rain beating against her window and wind

7
00:00:40.142 --> 00:00:44.844
howling. Mary sat up in bed and felt miserable and angry.

8
00:00:44.864 --> 00:00:48.646
"The rain only came because it knew I didn't want it."

9
00:00:49.206 --> 00:00:50.887
She threw herself on her pillow.

10
00:00:52.407 --> 00:00:55.629
Though she tried to go back to sleep, the wind kept her awake.

11
00:00:56.869 --> 00:01:00.931
She had been lying awake for nearly an hour, when a sound made her sit up in bed.

12
00:01:00.951 --> 00:01:03.072
"That isn't the wind.

13
00:01:03.572 --> 00:01:05.254
It's that crying I heard before."

14
00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:10.438
The door of her room was open a little, and the sound came down the corridor.

15
00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:14.341
She had to find out who was crying!

16
00:01:15.702 --> 00:01:19.385
There was a candle by her bed, so she took that and stepped out of her room.

17
00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:25.450
She walked slowly along the corridors, letting the crying lead her on.

18
00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:32.115
Finally she came to the door covered with tapestry, the one she had found that day she was

19
00:01:32.195 --> 00:01:36.698
lost. She pushed it open and stood in the corridor.

20
00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:39.560
The crying was quite nearby now.

21
00:01:41.521 --> 00:01:43.823
Mary could see light coming from beneath a door.

22
00:01:45.023 --> 00:01:46.985
Whoever was crying was in that room.

23
00:01:48.226 --> 00:01:50.727
Mary took a deep breath and opened the door.

24
00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:53.769
She found herself in a big room.

25
00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:00.054
A fire glowed faintly on the hearth, and a light burned by the side of a bed.

26
00:02:01.855 --> 00:02:03.156
A boy was lying in the bed.

27
00:02:03.628 --> 00:02:06.410
He had a pale face with large, dark eyes.

28
00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:12.333
His hair tumbled over his forehead and made his thin face seem even smaller.

29
00:02:13.513 --> 00:02:15.535
Mary began to walk toward the bed.

30
00:02:15.555 --> 00:02:17.656
"Are you a ghost?"

31
00:02:18.136 --> 00:02:19.597
cried the boy in surprise.

32
00:02:19.617 --> 00:02:22.538
"No. Are you?"

33
00:02:23.079 --> 00:02:23.819
asked Mary.

34
00:02:23.839 --> 00:02:28.582
"No. I'm Colin Craven," replied the boy.

35
00:02:28.602 --> 00:02:29.582
"Who are you?"

36
00:02:29.602 --> 00:02:32.384
"I'm Mary Lennox.

37
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.201
Mr. Craven is my uncle."

38
00:02:35.441 --> 00:02:37.402
"He's my father," said the boy.

39
00:02:37.422 --> 00:02:39.383
"Your father!"

40
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:40.703
gasped Mary.

41
00:02:40.723 --> 00:02:43.325
"No one ever told me that Mr.

42
00:02:43.385 --> 00:02:44.405
Craven had a son!

43
00:02:45.345 --> 00:02:47.186
Did anyone ever tell you that I live here?"

44
00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:50.007
The boy shook his head.

45
00:02:50.027 --> 00:02:52.468
"They didn't dare tell me that.

46
00:02:53.369 --> 00:02:56.030
They knew I'd be afraid you would see me and talk about me."

47
00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:00.672
"Why?" Mary felt more mystified every moment.

48
00:03:01.856 --> 00:03:03.918
"Because I'm always ill and lying in bed.

49
00:03:05.098 --> 00:03:08.461
If I live, I may be a hunchback, but I won't live.

50
00:03:09.742 --> 00:03:13.064
My father won't let anyone, not even the servants, talk about me."

51
00:03:13.084 --> 00:03:16.626
"Oh, what a strange house this is!"

52
00:03:17.187 --> 00:03:17.827
said Mary.

53
00:03:19.148 --> 00:03:21.910
"Rooms are locked up, and gardens are locked up.

54
00:03:22.891 --> 00:03:24.272
Have you been locked up too?"

55
00:03:24.292 --> 00:03:28.455
"No. I stay in this room because I don't want to be moved.

56
00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:30.136
It tires me too much."

57
00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.801
"Does your father come to see you?"

58
00:03:32.821 --> 00:03:36.764
"Sometimes when I'm asleep," said the boy.

59
00:03:36.784 --> 00:03:38.425
"He doesn't want to see me."

60
00:03:39.646 --> 00:03:41.007
Mary could not believe this.

61
00:03:41.027 --> 00:03:46.730
"Why?" "My mother died when I was born, and it makes him miserable to look at me.

62
00:03:47.931 --> 00:03:50.453
He thinks I don't know, but I've heard people talking.

63
00:03:50.953 --> 00:03:51.934
He almost hates me."

64
00:03:51.954 --> 00:03:57.278
"He hates the garden because she died," Mary said quietly.

65
00:03:57.298 --> 00:03:59.219
"What garden?"

66
00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:05.427
"Oh, just just a garden your mother liked," Mary said cautiously.

67
00:04:06.707 --> 00:04:08.308
She tried to change the subject.

68
00:04:08.328 --> 00:04:10.029
"Have you always been here?"

69
00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.893
Colin told her he used to be taken to the seaside, but he didn't like it when people

70
00:04:15.953 --> 00:04:16.513
stared at him.

71
00:04:17.814 --> 00:04:22.217
A doctor from London recommended that he go out in the fresh air, but he didn't like that

72
00:04:22.317 --> 00:04:27.740
either. He was allowed to have whatever he asked for, yet he still wasn't happy.

73
00:04:29.332 --> 00:04:33.896
"Everyone must do as I say or I get angry, and it makes me ill to be angry.

74
00:04:35.077 --> 00:04:36.938
No one believes I shall live to grow up."

75
00:04:38.219 --> 00:04:42.643
He said this as if he were so used to the idea that it didn't bother him anymore.

76
00:04:43.804 --> 00:04:47.507
He was more interested in asking Mary lots of questions about herself.

77
00:04:49.328 --> 00:04:54.533
As she talked about her life in India and the long voyage to England, he seemed to grow

78
00:04:54.633 --> 00:04:58.116
sleepy. But then he asked how old she was.

79
00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:04.577
"I'm ten, and so are you," said Mary, forgetting to be careful for a moment.

80
00:05:05.818 --> 00:05:06.678
"How do you know that?"

81
00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:09.120
Colin demanded in a surprised voice.

82
00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:15.606
"Because when you were born, the garden door was locked, and the key was buried.

83
00:05:16.547 --> 00:05:18.348
And it has been locked for ten years."

84
00:05:19.609 --> 00:05:20.870
Colin half sat up.

85
00:05:20.891 --> 00:05:23.533
"What garden door was locked?

86
00:05:23.953 --> 00:05:24.454
Who did it?

87
00:05:24.894 --> 00:05:25.995
Where was the key buried?"

88
00:05:26.375 --> 00:05:27.016
he exclaimed.

89
00:05:28.212 --> 00:05:30.839
Mary's heart suddenly began to pound.
